P3: stripInvalidXmlControls leaves XML 1.0-invalid code points that are not covered by the regex. The stripped ranges exclude `\uFFFE`, `\uFFFF`, and lone surrogates (`\uD800-\uDFFF`), which are not valid in XML 1.0. A `result.name` or message containing these would still produce malformed XML after `xml()` escapes only the five standard entities, so the reported redaction/sanitization guarantee in the JUnit output is not fully met. Extend the pattern to also drop `\uFFFE\uFFFF` (surrogates are generally not present in well-formed UTF-8 strings but are worth guarding).

