P2: The "missing-code" and "wrong-state" cases cannot actually detect a regression in the harness's code/state validation. The mock returns an empty JSON body for every token (and refresh) request, so validatedTokenSet throws and the flow reports "failed" regardless of whether the authorization callback was validated at all; the first fixture also has refreshAfterExchange: true, so even a valid token would fail at the (empty) refresh response. Since the only assertion is status === "failed", the test stays green even if validatedRedirectCode's code/state checks are removed, giving false confidence that these faults are caught. To make the faults meaningful, return a valid token set (access_token + token_type) and use a fixture without refreshAfterExchange (or return a valid refresh body), so a missed authorization fault lets the flow complete and fail the test instead of silently failing downstream.

